  "strategy": "We will use an enhanced coordinate descent approach combined with a refined update strategy to improve the quality of the solution found by the previous program. The key improvements are:\n\n1. Approach and Rationale:\n   We combine two methods: (a) enhanced coordinate descent to optimize individual coefficients while respecting the problem constraints, and (b) a smarter update strategy guided by projected gradients. This approach reduces the risk of getting stuck in local minima and accelerates convergence by using second-order information from the gradient.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n   - The goal is to minimize: \n         obj(a) = 2 * n * max(conv(a, a)) / (sum(a))^2\n     where a is the sequence of n non-negative coefficients.\n   - For each coordinate, we first compute the gradient projection to determine the most promising region before optimizing it with golden section search.\n   - The last element of the sequence is always pinned to 0.0 to enforce the non-decreasing constraint at the tails and increase numerical stability.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n   - We start with an initial sequence either from the 'height_sequence_1' global variable (if available) or a diverse initialization.\n   - For each coordinate, try to balance the sequence elements to form a more symmetrical pattern.\n   - Use the gradient direction (from the previous evaluation) to focus the search on regions that have improved the objective in the past.\n   - Keep track of the best candidate seen throughout the search.\n   - Ensure all updates maintain non-negative values and finite-positive sums.\n\n4. Detailed Steps:\n   - If 'height_sequence_1' is available and valid, use it as an initial candidate. Otherwise, fall back to a diverse initialization.\n   - For optimization, use the following steps:\n        1. Compute the gradient (if available) of the objective with respect to the coordinate.\n        2. Choose direction samples based on the gradient.\n        3. Use a hybrid of golden section and smarter direction updates.\n        4. Avoid the smoothing used in previous attempts to prevent numerical issues.\n   - Every update must be validated with the original objective function.\n   - We allow up to 2*n exploration per coordinate but watch for stability.\n\n5. Search Choices:\n   - The initial length is optimized by the program.\n   - We use a step size of 0.1 to 1.0 in the golden section search to balance exploration and exploitation.\n   - The algorithm schedules expensive evaluations based on the function's impact on the objective.\n\n6. Compute Budget and Robustness:\n   - The dominant cost is the convolution evaluation, so we cap the number of evaluations per coordinate.\n   - We use a safe fallback if updates prove unstable or violate non-negative constraints.\n   - Validate every update with a small time margin to prevent float-point drift from accumulating.",
